      "rev_n_1_17037e_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_1_17037e_0",
        "node_id": "n_1_17037e",
        "thought_text": "Model the clearings as nodes and paths as edges in a graph. Since the friend moves randomly to any adjacent node with equal probability, this is a random walk problem. The goal is to find the expected time to reach node N-1 starting from node 0. We can set up a system of linear equations where E[u] is the expected time from node u. For the exit node N-1, E[N-1] = 0. For any other node u, E[u] = 1 + (1/deg(u)) * sum(E[v]) for all neighbors v of u. Solve this system using Gaussian elimination or substitution.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "root",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794590933.5007913
      },
      "rev_n_2_3c54d1_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_2_3c54d1_0",
        "node_id": "n_2_3c54d1",
        "thought_text": "Given the small constraint N <= 20, the system of linear equations approach is computationally feasible. The matrix will be of size (N-1)x(N-1) since we don't need an equation for the exit node. We can construct the matrix A and vector b such that A*x = b, where x contains the expected times for nodes 0 to N-2. Then solve for x[0]. This method is exact and handles the 'randomly running around' condition perfectly by using the degree of each node as the denominator.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "root",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794590933.5008066
      },
      "rev_n_3_683a96_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_3_683a96_0",
        "node_id": "n_3_683a96",
        "thought_text": "Alternatively, since N is very small, we could simulate the random walk many times (Monte Carlo simulation) to estimate the average time. However, simulation might not achieve the required precision of 10^-5 without an extremely large number of trials, and it doesn't guarantee an exact analytical solution. The linear equation method is superior here because it provides an exact answer efficiently.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "root",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794590933.5008147
      },
      "rev_n_4_2ef431_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_4_2ef431_0",
        "node_id": "n_4_2ef431",
        "thought_text": "Formulate a system of linear equations where E[u] represents the expected time to reach the exit from node u. For the exit node, E[N-1] = 0. For other nodes, E[u] = 1 + average(E[v]) over all neighbors v. Solve this system using Gaussian elimination given the small constraint N <= 20.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "8719145922418194845",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794590992.9071434
      },
      "rev_n_5_06f404_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_5_06f404_0",
        "node_id": "n_5_06f404",
        "thought_text": "Since N is very small (up to 20), implement a direct simulation approach with a large number of trials to estimate the expected time empirically, though this might be less precise than the analytical method.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "8719145922418194845",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794590992.9071608
      },
      "rev_n_6_2f6449_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_6_2f6449_0",
        "node_id": "n_6_2f6449",
        "thought_text": "Use dynamic programming with memoization and iterative relaxation (value iteration) to solve the system of equations, starting with an initial guess of 0 for all nodes and updating values until convergence.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "8719145922418194845",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794590992.9071703
      },
      "rev_n_7_231bc0_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_7_231bc0_0",
        "node_id": "n_7_231bc0",
        "thought_text": "Construct a system of linear equations where E[u] = 1 + sum(E[v]/deg(u)) for all non-exit nodes u, with E[N-1] = 0, then solve the resulting (N-1)x(N-1) matrix equation for E[0].",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "247184097645878218",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794591011.91079
      },
      "rev_n_8_68c751_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_8_68c751_0",
        "node_id": "n_8_68c751",
        "thought_text": "Use Gaussian elimination to solve the linear system derived from the random walk recurrence relations, which is efficient enough given N <= 20.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "247184097645878218",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794591011.9108047
      },
      "rev_n_9_f0ed80_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_9_f0ed80_0",
        "node_id": "n_9_f0ed80",
        "thought_text": "Simulate the random walk process many times (e.g., 10^6 iterations) to estimate the average time empirically, though this may lack the precision required compared to the analytical solution.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "247184097645878218",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794591011.9108124
      },
      "rev_n_10_950139_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_10_950139_0",
        "node_id": "n_10_950139",
        "thought_text": "Set up a system of linear equations where $E_i$ is the expected time to reach node $N-1$ from node $i$. For each node $i$, $E_i = 1 + \\frac{1}{deg(i)} \\sum_{j \\in neighbors(i)} E_j$, with $E_{N-1} = 0$. Solve this system using Gaussian elimination.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "9054008671611838143",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794591047.474434
      },
      "rev_n_11_6a5010_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_11_6a5010_0",
        "node_id": "n_11_6a5010",
        "thought_text": "Use dynamic programming with memoization. Define a recursive function `solve(u)` that returns the expected time from `u`. If `u` is the exit, return 0. Otherwise, return $1 + \\text{average}(\\text{solve}(v))$ for all neighbors $v$. Use a dictionary to cache results to avoid re-computation.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "9054008671611838143",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794591047.4744523
      },
      "rev_n_12_e3ea6c_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_12_e3ea6c_0",
        "node_id": "n_12_e3ea6c",
        "thought_text": "Since $N$ is very small ($\\le 20$), perform a Monte Carlo simulation by running the random walk thousands of times. Track the average steps taken to reach node $N-1$. While less precise than the analytical method, it might suffice if the number of trials is large enough, though it risks failing the $10^{-5}$ precision requirement.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "9054008671611838143",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794591047.47446
      },
      "rev_n_11_6a5010_1": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_11_6a5010_1",
        "node_id": "n_11_6a5010",
        "thought_text": "Thought-2 (replaced): The naive recursive approach with simple memoization fails because the graph contains cycles, causing infinite recursion before the system of linear equations is resolved. Instead, we should set up a system of linear equations where $E[u] = 1 + \\frac{1}{\\text{deg}(u)} \\sum E[v]$ for non-target nodes and $E[\\text{target}] = 0$, then solve this system using Gaussian elimination to find the exact expected time.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "9054008671611838143",
        "replaces_revision_id": "rev_n_11_6a5010_0",
        "feedback_id": "fb_56dab5aca6",
        "created_at": 1794591140.7895935
      },
      "rev_n_13_99a9b4_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_13_99a9b4_0",
        "node_id": "n_13_99a9b4",
        "thought_text": "Formulate a system of linear equations where E[u] is the expected time from node u to the exit. For the exit node N-1, E[N-1] = 0. For any other node u, the equation is E[u] = 1 + (1/deg(u)) * sum(E[v]) for all neighbors v. This creates a system of N linear equations with N variables that can be solved using Gaussian elimination.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "214530126670889197",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794591162.9119654
      },
      "rev_n_14_337091_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_14_337091_0",
        "node_id": "n_14_337091",
        "thought_text": "Since N is small (<= 20), implement Gaussian elimination directly on the coefficient matrix derived from the random walk equations. Construct the matrix A where A[i][i] = 1 - 1/deg(i) and A[i][j] = -1/deg(i) for neighbors j, then solve Ax = b where b is a vector of 1s (except for the exit row).",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "214530126670889197",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794591162.9119806
      },
      "rev_n_15_822bf3_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_15_822bf3_0",
        "node_id": "n_15_822bf3",
        "thought_text": "Consider using iterative methods like Value Iteration or Gauss-Seidel instead of direct Gaussian elimination. Initialize E[N-1]=0 and all other E[u]=infinity, then repeatedly update E[u] = 1 + average(E[v]) until convergence. This avoids matrix inversion and might be simpler to code, though Gaussian elimination is more precise for small N.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "214530126670889197",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794591162.9119892
      },
      "rev_n_16_1bb751_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_16_1bb751_0",
        "node_id": "n_16_1bb751",
        "thought_text": "Construct a system of linear equations based on the property that the expected time from a node u is 1 plus the average of expected times from its neighbors, then solve the (N-1)x(N-1) matrix for the starting node.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "-3933243120231534330",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794591181.418737
      },
      "rev_n_17_7d389b_0": {
